update articles set
  title_en = $t$Customs Rules Guide: Cash, Food, Duty-Free and Medicines$t$,
  excerpt_en = $e$How customs works worldwide: duty-free allowances, cash declaration limits, food, plant and medicine rules, banned items and driving across borders.$e$,
  body_en = $b$<p>Customs is the part of the journey most travellers think about least, until a suitcase is opened at the airport or a ferry port. Customs rules decide what you can bring into a country, what you must declare and what is prohibited altogether. Getting them wrong can mean confiscated goods, fines or long delays. This guide explains the principles that apply almost everywhere, uses the European Union as a worked example, compares cash declaration rules in major destinations, and covers food, medicines and items that are commonly banned.</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> customs allowances and thresholds are set by law and change from time to time. The figures below reflect commonly published rules, but always check the official customs authority of the country you are entering before you travel.</p>

<h2>How Customs Works: The Basics</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Green channel ("nothing to declare"):</strong> use it only if everything you carry is within the duty-free allowances and none of it is restricted.</li>
<li><strong>Red channel ("goods to declare"):</strong> use it if you exceed allowances, carry restricted items or carry cash above the declaration threshold. Declaring is not an admission of wrongdoing. Failing to declare is what leads to penalties.</li>
<li><strong>Declaration forms and apps:</strong> some countries, such as the United States, Australia and New Zealand, ask every arriving traveller to complete a declaration, on paper or digitally. Answer the food and cash questions honestly.</li>
<li><strong>Random checks:</strong> customs officers may inspect any traveller in any channel.</li>
<li><strong>Personal use vs commercial:</strong> allowances apply to goods for your own use or as gifts. Large quantities of the same item may be treated as commercial goods and taxed or refused.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cash and Currency Rules</h2>
<p>Many countries require you to declare large amounts of cash, not because carrying it is illegal, but to fight money laundering. The table shows commonly published thresholds. In most cases they include other currencies at their equivalent value and certain bearer instruments such as traveller's cheques.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Destination</th><th>Declare when carrying</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>European Union</td><td>10,000 EUR or more, entering or leaving the EU</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>10,000 GBP or more, entering or leaving from outside the UK</td></tr>
<tr><td>United States</td><td>more than 10,000 USD, entering or leaving</td></tr>
<tr><td>Canada</td><td>10,000 CAD or more</td></tr>
<tr><td>Australia</td><td>10,000 AUD or more</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Arab Emirates</td><td>60,000 AED or more</td></tr>
<tr><td>Singapore</td><td>more than 20,000 SGD</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Thresholds usually apply per person, and some countries treat families travelling together as one unit. A few countries, such as Morocco, also limit how much of their own currency you can take in or out. Keep exchange receipts and bank withdrawal slips, especially when carrying larger sums.</p>

<h2>Duty-Free Allowances: The EU as an Example</h2>
<p>Every country sets its own allowances for alcohol, tobacco and other goods. The EU is a useful example because its rules cover 27 countries. When you arrive in an EU country from outside the EU, for example from the UK, the USA, Turkey or the UAE, the following <strong>commonly cited EU allowances</strong> apply for travellers aged 17 and over. Some member states set lower limits for land and sea borders, so check the country you are entering.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical EU allowance</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Cigarettes</td><td>200 cigarettes (some countries apply a lower limit at land borders)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spirits over 22%</td><td>1 litre, or 2 litres of drinks up to 22%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Still wine</td><td>4 litres</td></tr>
<tr><td>Beer</td><td>16 litres</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other goods (perfume, electronics, gifts)</td><td>up to 430 EUR in value by air or sea, and 300 EUR by land. Lower limits apply to children in some countries.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Goods above these allowances must be declared, and import VAT and duties may apply. You cannot split the value of one expensive item between several people. Other countries work on the same principle with different figures. The UK, for example, has its own allowances since leaving the EU, and the US, Canada and Australia each publish theirs online.</p>

<h2>Food, Plants and Animal Products</h2>
<p>This is the area where travellers most often get caught out, usually with homemade food, snacks from the plane or fruit picked up on the way.</p>
<h3>General Principles</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meat and dairy:</strong> many countries restrict or ban personal imports of meat, sausages, milk and cheese from abroad to protect animal health. The EU bans them in principle from most non-EU countries, with limited exceptions such as certain baby food and medical foods.</li>
<li><strong>Fruit, vegetables and plants:</strong> fresh produce, plants, cuttings and seeds often need a phytosanitary certificate. Under EU rules a few fruits are exempt, including bananas, pineapples, coconuts, dates and durians.</li>
<li><strong>Usually allowed in personal quantities:</strong> packaged sweets and biscuits, bread, tea, coffee, spices and sealed oils, provided they don't contain meat or dairy. Rules vary, so check if you are unsure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Strict Biosecurity Countries</h3>
<p>Australia and New Zealand have some of the strictest biosecurity rules in the world, and the United States is also strict about meat, fruit and plants. Declare all food, plant material and animal products on arrival, even if you think they are allowed. Declared items that are not permitted are usually just disposed of, while undeclared items can lead to fines.</p>

<h2>Medicines</h2>
<ul>
<li>Carry medicines in their original packaging with a copy of your prescription, preferably translated or with the generic name.</li>
<li>Bring only the quantity you need for your trip.</li>
<li>Some medicines are controlled or banned in certain countries. The UAE, Saudi Arabia, Japan and Singapore, for example, restrict some painkillers, sedatives, ADHD medicines, decongestants or medicines containing codeine, and may require a permit in advance. Check the destination's official health authority or embassy website before travelling.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Items That Are Commonly Prohibited or Restricted</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Counterfeit goods:</strong> fake designer items can be seized in the EU, the US and elsewhere, and you may be fined.</li>
<li><strong>Protected species:</strong> products made from protected animals or plants, such as some corals, shells, ivory, tortoiseshell and certain reptile skins, are covered by international CITES rules.</li>
<li><strong>Cultural goods and antiques:</strong> exporting archaeological objects, fossils or antiques usually requires a permit. Buy only from reputable sellers who can provide paperwork.</li>
<li><strong>Weapons and replicas:</strong> including some knives, pepper sprays and toy guns that look real.</li>
<li><strong>E-cigarettes and other items:</strong> some countries ban items that are ordinary elsewhere. Singapore and Thailand, for example, prohibit vapes, and Singapore restricts chewing gum.</li>
<li><strong>Drones:</strong> many countries have registration or authorisation rules, and some, such as Morocco, generally require prior authorisation to bring a drone in at all.</li>
<li><strong>Drugs:</strong> cannabis and other narcotics are illegal to transport across borders, regardless of the laws in your home or destination country.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Travelling by Car and Ferry</h2>
<p>Driving across borders, for example between EU countries and the UK, or on ferry routes such as Spain to Morocco, brings a few extra points to check.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vehicle documents:</strong> carry registration and insurance papers. Some countries, Morocco among them, only admit foreign-registered vehicles temporarily, with conditions and time limits.</li>
<li><strong>Insurance:</strong> make sure your policy covers every country on your route, or buy border insurance where it is offered.</li>
<li><strong>Goods in the car:</strong> gifts and household items are common, but large quantities of new goods (electronics, appliances, clothing still with tags) may be considered commercial and taxed.</li>
<li><strong>Allowances still apply:</strong> food, tobacco and alcohol limits apply at land borders and ferry ports too, and are sometimes lower than at airports.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tax-Free Shopping and VAT Refunds</h2>
<p>Visitors residing outside the EU can often reclaim VAT on purchases made in EU shops that offer tax-free shopping, provided the goods leave the EU in their luggage. Ask for the tax-free form in the shop, keep receipts and have the form validated by customs at your final EU exit point before checking your bags. Many other countries, including Japan, Singapore and Australia, run their own tourist tax refund schemes, while the UK ended VAT-free shopping for most visitors in 2021. Minimum purchase amounts and procedures vary.</p>

<h2>Practical Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Check the official customs website of each country you will enter, including transit countries if you collect your luggage.</li>
<li>Keep receipts for expensive items, especially electronics and jewellery you take abroad, to prove you already owned them.</li>
<li>Pack medicines in your hand luggage with prescriptions.</li>
<li>Leave meat and dairy products behind unless you have confirmed they are allowed.</li>
<li>Declare cash above the threshold, and if in doubt, declare.</li>
<li>Never carry a package for someone else without knowing exactly what it contains.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Can I bring cheese or cured meat into another country?</h3>
<p>Often not. The EU, the US, Australia and many other countries ban or restrict personal imports of meat and dairy from abroad, and these items are regularly confiscated at airports and ports. Check the rules for your destination.</p>
<h3>How much cash can I carry when I travel?</h3>
<p>Carrying cash is generally legal, but you must declare amounts above the local threshold, such as 10,000 EUR in the EU or more than 10,000 USD in the US. Some countries also limit their own currency, so check official sources before you travel.</p>
<h3>Do I need to declare gifts?</h3>
<p>Gifts count towards your duty-free allowance. If their total value exceeds the allowance, declare them.</p>
<h3>What happens if I forget to declare something?</h3>
<p>Goods may be seized, and you may have to pay duties plus a fine. In serious cases, especially involving cash or prohibited items, legal proceedings can follow.</p>
<h3>Can I bring spices, tea and sealed oils home?</h3>
<p>Usually yes, in personal quantities, provided they are commercially packaged. Countries with strict biosecurity may still ask you to declare them. If you are flying, follow cabin liquid rules or pack oils in checked luggage.</p>

<h2>Travel Smoothly with Fastamor</h2>
<p>Preparing for customs is part of a stress-free journey. The <strong>Fastamor AI assistant</strong> can help you build a packing and documents checklist for your route, find flights from your city and point you to the official sources to check. Tell Fastamor where you are travelling and plan your trip with confidence.</p>$b$,
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'customs-regulations-guide' and status = 'published';
